{
"A phone tower stands on top of the hill.": "c56de355983c",
"A phone works by sending and receiving radio waves. Radio waves are a kind of light that our eyes cannot see, and they travel at the speed of light, about 300,000 kilometers every second. Your phone turns your voice into a pattern of tiny changes in a radio wave, and its antenna sends the wave out in all directions. A tower three kilometers away receives it in about ten millionths of a second.": "9bdf7911d37a",
"A stone dropped in a pond makes waves in every direction.": "2ead6bd7fe79",
"Each radio station uses its own frequency.": "1e61e310ec88",
"In 1901, the Italian inventor Guglielmo Marconi sent a radio signal across the Atlantic Ocean, about 3,400 kilometers from England to Canada. The whole message was the letter S in Morse code. In 1973, Martin Cooper of Motorola made the first call from a handheld mobile phone, standing on a street in New York. His phone weighed more than a kilogram. Today's phones are far lighter, but they still use the same invisible waves.": "d1bfc47b6417",
"In an emergency, stay calm and call for help.": "e75ebb7f99d7",
"My wireless earbuds connect to my phone.": "5b2621a278b9",
"Radio waves grow weaker as they spread out, and they lose even more strength when something solid is in the way. They can bend a little around a hilltop, but a big mountain blocks most of the signal. That is why hikers often have signal on a summit but none in the valley behind it. Lower-frequency waves travel farther and get around obstacles better. Higher-frequency waves, like the 3.5 gigahertz used for 5G, carry more data but do not reach as far, so 5G needs more base stations.": "2fda5ccbdaf7",
"Taiwan's first 5G service began on June 30, 2020. By the end of 2025, Taiwan had more than 120,000 licensed base stations, and for the first time more of them were for 5G than for 4G. Still, nobody can put a tower behind every mountain. In an emergency, dial 112. If any phone company's base station can hear your phone, the call goes through, even without a SIM card. On long hikes, carry another way to call for help too, such as a satellite messenger.": "f94f8aacc2a7",
"The radio has a long metal antenna.": "1c3d19f6fa14",
"The tall building blocks our view of the sea.": "806e9da4359c",
"The waves do not travel all the way to your friend. They only go as far as the nearest base station, a tower with antennas on a hill, a rooftop, or a pole. The base station connects to the cable network from Lesson 5, which carries the call the rest of the way. Each base station covers an area called a cell, which is why Americans say cell phone. As you move, your phone switches from one base station to the next without dropping the call. This switch is called a handoff.": "7c52a9c94503",
"There is no phone signal in the tunnel.": "5b12395f9769",
"Where do the radio waves from your phone go first, and what happens to the call after that?": "867776a9fbb1",
"Why can you sometimes get a signal on a mountaintop but not in the valley behind it?": "88f80ead59bf",
"Why does 5G need more base stations than older networks?": "493e9eef4786",
"You are hiking in Taiwan's mountains with your family. In the parking lot, your phone shows full bars. An hour up the trail, behind a ridge, the bars drop to one, and then to none. The phone has not changed, and neither has the network. What changed is the mountain between you and a tower you cannot see. How does a phone talk through thin air, and why can a mountain stop it?": "3082dc06ab52",
